 "János Sarusi Kis"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10115"} "István Vaskuti"] ", the latter of whom was a 1980 Olympic champion, had won the C-2 500 at the 1985 and 1986 World Championships, but the Polish duo of " [:a {:href "/athletes/10471"} "Marek Dopierała"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10502"} "Marek Łbik"] ", the 1985 runners-up, had stolen that title in 1987. Poland won its heat in the opening round, but Hungary came in third to " [:a {:href "/athletes/9847"} "Alexander Schuck"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10004"} "Thomas Zereske"] " of East Germany and the Soviet Union’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/11007"} "Victor Reneischi"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/11026"} "Nicolae Juravschi"] ". Bulgaria’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9187"} "Deyan Bonev"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9186"} "Petar Bozhilov"] " took a third heat. East Germany won its heat again in the semi-finals, but Poland was defeated by the Soviet Union and Bulgaria came in third to Hungary and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9672"} "Philippe Renaud"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9597"} "Joël Bettin"] " of France. The Soviet Union won the gold medal in the final in Olympic record time and became double Olympic champions the next day when they won the C-2 1000. The race for silver came down to a battle between France and Poland, with the latter emerging victorious by a margin of only 0.2 seconds. Poland too would win another Olympic medal in the C-2 1000, but theirs would be bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "396/394"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/11007"} "Victor Reneischi"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/11026"} "Nicolae Juravschi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olymped